The 5 seconds cover the whole exchange, including reading the body, so a slow download can time out inside `res.json()` as well. Keep the body read inside the same `try`.

`AbortSignal.timeout` needs Node 17.3+ or a browser from 2022 onwards (Safari 16+).

```js
function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    const remaining = wait - (Date.now() - last);
    clearTimeout(timer);
    if (remaining <= 0) {
      last = Date.now();
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else {
      // Trailing call, so the final scroll position is not missed.
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        last = Date.now();
        fn.apply(this, args);
      }, remaining);
    }
  };
}

window.addEventListener("scroll", throttle(onScroll, 200), { passive: true });
```

It runs immediately on the first event, then at most once per 200 ms, with one last call after scrolling stops.

To remove the listener later, keep a reference to the throttled function; calling `throttle(onScroll, 200)` again creates a different one.

If lodash is already in the project, `_.throttle(onScroll, 200)` behaves the same way.
